WEBVTT

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:27.840
<v Ira Glass>OK, so we're going to try something different today. Something we have never tried on our show. And I got the idea for this from a conversation that I had with Jiayang Fan. Jiayang did a story for our show a little while back. And she's not somebody who's normally on the radio. She's a magazine writer.
<v Ira Glass>好的，今天我们尝试点不一样的。这是我们节目中从未尝试过的。这个点子源于我和贾扬·范（Jiayang Fan）的一次对话。贾扬前阵子为我们的节目写了一个故事。她通常不在电台工作，她是位杂志撰稿人。

00:00:27.840 --> 00:01:01.310
<v Ira Glass>And so when time came for her to record her script, she sat in the studio with one of our producers, Diane Wu, who had her read. And you know, it's a weird thing to sound, like, relaxed and natural while reading words off a page in a totally soundproof room, hearing your voice on headphones. And Diane did with Jiayang what we always do. She'd tell her, you know, go back and do that sentence a little looser. Or you know, underline this word or whatever. And that just hit something in Jiayang in this way that doesn't usually happen.
<v Ira Glass>当她开始录制脚本时，她和我们的制作人黛安·吴（Diane Wu）坐在录音室里，黛安负责指导她朗读。你知道，在一个完全隔音的房间里，戴着耳机听自己的声音，同时还要读着纸上的文字，却要听起来轻松自然，这其实是很奇怪的感觉。黛安对贾扬做着我们平时会做的事情。她会告诉她：“再来一遍，那句话读得再放松一点，”或者“给这个词加个重音”之类的。这在某种程度上触动了贾扬，产生了不同寻常的效果。

00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:27.780
<v Jiayang Fan>Having a fellow Asian-American who looks exactly like me coach me through that process, hearing exactly how much more American she sounded than me, it made me think anew about my voice and the way I speak English.
<v Jiayang Fan>让一位长相和我一模一样的亚裔美国同胞来指导我完成这个过程，听着她比我听起来“美国味”浓得多，这让我重新思考了自己的声音以及我说英语的方式。

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:41.880
<v Ira Glass>OK. I have a bunch of things to say to that. First of all, this idea that you and Diane look exactly the same, that's racist. You do not.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，对此我有很多话想说。首先，关于你和黛安长得一模一样这种想法，这可是种族主义。你们长得根本不像。

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.650
<v Jiayang Fan>Fair point. Fair point.
<v Jiayang Fan>有道理。说得对。

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:46.530
<v Ira Glass>And then it's interesting to me that you perceived it as--
<v Ira Glass>而且我很有兴趣知道，你为什么会觉得……

00:01:46.530 --> 00:02:07.350
<v Ira Glass>Jiayang has thought a lot about the way that she speaks English and the way that others do, going back to when she was seven years old and she came to the United States from China. This was in the '90s in a mostly white part of New Haven. And it was just understood, she said, that it was her job in the family to learn to speak perfect, accentless English as soon as possible.
<v Ira Glass>贾扬对自己说英语的方式以及他人的表达方式思考了很多，这要追溯到她七岁从中国来到美国的时候。那是90年代，她住在纽黑文一个大部分是白人的社区。她说，当时家里默认的一件事就是：她作为家庭成员的职责，就是尽快学会说出完美、没有口音的英语。

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:51.450
<v Jiayang Fan>And my mom and some of her peers had said, oh, you have this very narrow window in which your tongue can adjust to the new language or not, because Chinese is so different than English. And I really-- because I was seven or eight at the time, I really didn't even think of it as in the brain. I thought about it as the rigidity of my tongue. And I was so afraid of my tongue literally hardening in my mouth and not being able to contort to this language. And I think it immediately becomes a test of your ability to survive and thrive in this new environment.
<v Jiayang Fan>我妈和她的一些同龄人曾说，调整舌头以适应新语言的黄金期非常短暂，因为中文和英语差别太大了。我当时才七八岁，甚至没觉得这是大脑的问题，我以为是舌头太僵硬了。我非常害怕我的舌头在嘴里真的变硬，以至于无法适应这种语言。我觉得这瞬间变成了一种在陌生环境生存和发展的能力测试。

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:53.430
<v Ira Glass>How well you can speak without an accent.
<v Ira Glass>看你能在没有口音的情况下说得多好。

00:02:53.430 --> 00:03:06.440
<v Jiayang Fan>Right. Like I've been told, OK, look, you're going to be living in water for the rest of your life. Well, the better you can swim, kind of-- I better get a few different kinds of strokes under my belt.
<v Jiayang Fan>没错。就像有人对我说：“好了，你看，你这辈子都得在水里生活了。嗯，你游得越好……我最好多学几种游泳姿势。”

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:11.930
<v Ira Glass>And when you view that whole attitude now, like, how do you see it?
<v Ira Glass>现在回看这种心态，你觉得如何？

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:35.240
<v Jiayang Fan>The adult me doesn't necessarily endorse the goal of speaking accentless English and the need to fit into the perfect American ideal, whatever that is. But that was what my parents thought was necessary to protect me in a foreign country.
<v Jiayang Fan>现在的我并不一定认同那种“必须说纯正地道英语”以及“需要融入完美美国理想（不管那是什么）”的目标。但那是当时我父母认为为了在外国保护我所必需做的事情。

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:54.590
<v Ira Glass>Jiayang did the hard work of mastering the countless little subtleties of pronunciation and usage to the point where she thought she sounded more or less like native English speakers. Friends, especially white friends, told her she didn't have an accent. And when she herself had little twinges of like, oh, maybe I sound different, she shrugged it off as being too hard on herself.
<v Ira Glass>贾扬付出了艰辛的努力，掌握了无数细微的发音和用法，以至于她认为自己听起来和母语人士差不多了。朋友们，特别是白人朋友，告诉她她没有口音。当她自己偶尔感到“哦，我可能听起来有点不一样”时，她也只是耸耸肩，觉得是自己对自己太苛刻了。

00:03:54.590 --> 00:04:12.840
<v Jiayang Fan>And hearing my voice on the radio, hearing myself speak, it was a very rude awakening. It was embarrassing and slightly mortifying. Like, I do sound different, even when I'm trying really, really hard not to.
<v Jiayang Fan>而在电台里听到自己的声音，听到自己说话，那是一种非常粗暴的觉醒。那让我感到尴尬，甚至有些羞愧。就像，“我确实听起来不一样，即使我非常非常努力地想要避免。”

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:21.010
<v Ira Glass>To be clear, the difference that she heard-- it wasn't that she had an accent like you normally think of an accent. Specifically, what she was hearing was--
<v Ira Glass>说清楚一点，她听到的那种区别——并不是通常认为的那种口音。具体来说，她听到的是……

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:44.640
<v Jiayang Fan>Over-enunciation. And maybe I was unattuned to how much effort there is in my most casual speech. A huge part of how I sound different is I'm so afraid that if I don't make the sound explicit, I won't be understood. And that process of making it explicit is what actually makes me sound really different, whereas--
<v Jiayang Fan>过度发音（Over-enunciation）。也许我没有意识到我在日常谈话中投入了多少力气。我听起来不同的很大一部分原因，是我太害怕如果我不把声音发得足够清晰，别人就听不懂。而那种刻意追求清晰的过程，恰恰让我听起来非常不同，而……

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:51.670
<v Ira Glass>It's funny. As you're saying this sentence, I feel so aware of how every final T and every final D, you're actually enunciating.
<v Ira Glass>很有趣。当你现在说这句话时，我注意到你每一个词尾的T和D都在用力发音。

00:04:51.670 --> 00:05:00.500
<v Jiayang Fan>Right. That effortfulness is part of how I speak.
<v Jiayang Fan>没错。那种用力感正是我的说话方式的一部分。

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:30.560
<v Ira Glass>OK, so we're finally getting to the reason that I'm telling you all this. One night a little while back, I ran into Jiayang at an event. And she explained all this to me and told me how since coming on our show, she was feeling self-conscious about her English in a way that she hadn't for years. But also and more importantly, she was finding herself listening to Chinese-American friends with new ears. Suddenly she was hearing the huge variations in cadence and speech that can give away that you're not a native speaker .
<v Ira Glass>好了，终于说到我告诉你们这些的原因了。不久前的一天晚上，我在一个活动上遇到了贾扬。她向我解释了这一切，并告诉我，自从上了我们的节目，她对自己的英语感到了一种多年未有的局促。更重要的是，她发现自己开始用全新的耳朵去倾听美籍华裔朋友的谈话。她突然听出了说话节奏和表达上的巨大差异，那些差异会让人听出你不是母语人士。

00:05:30.560 --> 00:06:02.910
<v Jiayang Fan>It would come out in the stringing together of certain words. And I became convinced that I could tell how old someone was when they arrived in this country and started speaking English. Because the more fluidly they could pronounce certain words or ride out a cadence, the younger they must have been when they arrived.
<v Jiayang Fan>这会体现在某些词汇的连读上。我变得确信，我能听出一个人几岁来到这个国家开始说英语。因为他们发音某些词汇或驾驭节奏越流畅，他们来到这里时就一定越年轻。

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:12.560
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, I remember that you said to me that night if you could just hear somebody speak, you would be able to tell them how long they had been in this country or how old they were when they arrived in this country.
<v Ira Glass>是的，我记得那天晚上你跟我说，只要听到某人说话，你就能说出他们在该国居住了多久，或者他们来到该国时有多大。

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:14.840
<v Jiayang Fan>Right. Their age of arrival.
<v Jiayang Fan>没错。也就是他们的抵达年龄。

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:36.470
<v Ira Glass>And I said to you at the time, challenge accepted. Do you remember? We were sitting there. I said, if I find let's say three people who moved to this country, could we have you come on the radio and you would guess how old they were when they arrived? And you could exhibit your superpower. And we would find out if, in fact, you were correct.
<v Ira Glass>我当时对你说：“挑战接受。”你还记得吗？我们当时坐在那儿。我说，如果我找来三位移民到这个国家的人，你能来电台猜猜他们抵达时的年龄吗？你可以展示你的超能力。我们会验证你是否准确。

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:52.260
<v Jiayang Fan>Exactly. And that I could hear in their speech something that they themselves didn't even necessarily hear. And that's what made it sort of magical to me.
<v Jiayang Fan>没错。我可以从他们的谈话中听出一些他们自己甚至没有察觉的东西。这对我来说有一种魔力。

00:06:52.260 --> 00:07:06.720
<v Ira Glass>And so that is what we're here to do today. Jiayang is going to play that game. Maybe the only way she could ever find out once and for all if indeed she can do this. We've created an entire radio game show for that purpose. Let me hear some studio audience, please.
<v Ira Glass>所以这就是我们今天要做的。贾扬要玩这个游戏。这可能是她唯一能一劳永逸地验证自己是否真有这种能力的方法。我们为此专门制作了一个电台游戏节目。请让我听听现场观众的声音。

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:09.440
<v Ira Glass>[APPLAUSE]
<v Ira Glass>[掌声]

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:40.630
<v Ira Glass>And then after doing that, we tried to figure out other experiences that people have that would best be captured not the way we usually do it on our show-- following people around with microphones and recording them, interviewing them-- but in game show format. So we are very excited to bring you something we have never heard of anybody trying, namely, an entire program of stories done as game shows. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>在那之后，我们试图找出人们的其他经历，这些经历最好不是通过我们通常的方式——带着麦克风跟拍、采访——而是以游戏节目的形式呈现。所以，我们非常兴奋地为你们带来一个我们从未听说过有人尝试过的节目，即整个节目内容都以游戏形式呈现。来自芝加哥WBEZ，这里是《This American Life》。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。请继续收听。

00:07:40.630 --> 00:07:48.010
<v Ira Glass>[APPLAUSE]
<v Ira Glass>[掌声]

00:07:48.010 --> 00:08:12.090
<v Ira Glass>Act 1, With Great Power Comes Great Pronounceability. OK, so we came up with this game to test Jiayang's claim. And Tobin Low, one of the editors on our program-- he's also Chinese-American-- got excited about the idea and volunteered to be our Bob Barker, our Alex Trebek for the game. And so I hand it off to him.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，强大的发音能力。好了，我们设计了这个游戏来测试贾扬的说法。托宾·洛（Tobin Low），我们节目的编辑之一——他也是美籍华裔——对这个想法很兴奋，并自愿担任我们的鲍勃·巴克（Bob Barker）和亚历克斯·特雷贝克（Alex Trebek）。所以我把话筒交给他。

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:16.590
<v Tobin Low>Welcome, ladies and gentlemen. I am Tobin Low. Hello, audience.
<v Tobin Low>女士们、先生们，欢迎光临。我是托宾·洛。大家好，观众们。

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:20.080
<v Tobin Low>[APPLAUSE]
<v Tobin Low>[掌声]

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:33.150
<v Tobin Low>Welcome to a game we are calling Date of Arrival, in which Jiayang Fan tells you when you immigrated to the United States. Jiayang, welcome. How are you feeling? Are you feeling confident?
<v Tobin Low>欢迎来到我们称之为“抵达日期”的游戏，在这个游戏中，贾扬·范要猜出你是什么时候移民到美国的。贾扬，欢迎。感觉如何？有信心吗？

00:08:33.150 --> 00:08:46.670
<v Jiayang Fan>I'm feeling a bit of trepidation, I have to admit. I was pumped for this, but now that the date has arrived, my Asian fear of failure is kicking in.
<v Jiayang Fan>说实话，我有点忐忑。我本来很兴奋，但现在时间到了，我的亚裔式失败恐惧症开始发作了。

00:08:46.670 --> 00:09:00.460
<v Tobin Low>Well, the good news is we have great guests lined up for you today. And if you guess two of them correctly, you're going to win our prize, which is a brand new car.
<v Tobin Low>好消息是，我们今天为你请到了很棒的嘉宾。如果你能猜对其中两个，你将赢得我们的奖品，一辆崭新的汽车。

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:08.240
<v Jiayang Fan>This only-- this only ups the stakes and my anxiety. I'm hoping that I can channel my anxiety for success.
<v Jiayang Fan>这只会……这只会增加赌注和我的焦虑。我希望我能把焦虑转化为成功的动力。

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:13.480
<v Tobin Low>It's all good. I have complete faith in you, Jiayang. Are you ready to hear the rules?
<v Tobin Low>没关系。我对你有十足的信心，贾扬。准备好听规则了吗？

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:14.380
<v Jiayang Fan>Hit me.
<v Jiayang Fan>放马过来吧。

00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:35.350
<v Tobin Low>OK. So each guest is somebody who immigrated to the United States from China at some point in their life. And they have been given up to three sentences to read aloud. So each sentence contains phrases like urban and rural America. And these are phrases that, Jiayang, you picked because they can be challenging to pronounce.
<v Tobin Low>好。每位嘉宾都是在人生某个阶段从中国移民到美国的。他们被要求朗读最多三句话。每句话都包含像“城市和乡村美国”这样的词组。贾扬，这些词组是你挑的，因为它们发音很有挑战性。

00:09:35.350 --> 00:09:51.370
<v Tobin Low>Or they are sentences that be very familiar to someone who's lived here for years. And at the end of each sentence, Jiayang, you get a chance to guess at what age they came to the US. If you are within one year of the correct age, we will give you the point. Does that make sense?
<v Tobin Low>或者它们是对于在这儿住过多年的人来说非常熟悉的句子。每句话结束时，贾扬，你有机会猜出他们来到美国时的年龄。如果你猜的年龄在正确年龄的一年误差范围内，我们就给你算一分。听明白了吗？

00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:55.000
<v Jiayang Fan>One year. I mean, that's narrow. But yes, I accept.
<v Jiayang Fan>一年。太严苛了吧。但好吧，我接受。

00:09:55.000 --> 00:10:04.960
<v Tobin Low>OK, great. Well, then we are going to welcome our first guest, Luke Ma. Hi, Luke, how are you? Where are you calling in from?
<v Tobin Low>好的，太棒了。那么，我们欢迎第一位嘉宾，卢克·马（Luke Ma）。嗨，卢克，你好吗？你从哪里打进来的？

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:09.130
<v Luke Ma>Hi, everyone. I'm doing lovely. I'm calling in from San Jose in California.
<v Luke Ma>大家好。我很好。我从加利福尼亚的圣何塞打来的。

00:10:09.130 --> 00:10:13.040
<v Tobin Low>Just for a point of reference for Jiayang, how old are you?
<v Tobin Low>为了给贾扬一个参考，你今年多大了？

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:15.490
<v Luke Ma>Oh, yes. I'm 41.
<v Luke Ma>哦，是的，我41岁。

00:10:15.490 --> 00:10:20.260
<v Tobin Low>All right. We are going to go ahead and have you read sentence number one.
<v Tobin Low>好的。我们要开始了，请你读第一句。

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:32.220
<v Luke Ma>All right. Here it goes. And I have to say, my Asian fear of failure is kicking in too. Now I feel like I must-- I have to stump-- I have to stump Fan Jiayang, otherwise I won't win.
<v Luke Ma>好的。开始了。我不得不说，我的亚裔失败恐惧症也发作了。现在我觉得我必须……我得难倒范贾扬，否则我赢不了。

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:34.060
<v Luke Ma>[LAUGHTER]
<v Luke Ma>[笑声]

00:10:34.060 --> 00:10:40.380
<v Luke Ma>So sentence one. The airplane flew over urban and rural America.
<v Luke Ma>那么第一句：“飞机飞过了城市和乡村的美国。”

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:43.990
<v Tobin Low>Jiayang, hearing sentence one, are you ready to make a guess?
<v Tobin Low>贾扬，听了第一句，准备好做出猜测了吗？

00:10:43.990 --> 00:10:49.750
<v Jiayang Fan>Not quite, not quite. I think I need a little-- I think I'm going to need a little bit more.
<v Jiayang Fan>还没完全准备好。我想我需要……我想我还需要更多一点。

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:50.680
<v Tobin Low>All right. Great.
<v Tobin Low>好的，太棒了。

00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:59.900
<v Luke Ma>Here you go. Sentence two. Got my hands up. They're playing my song. I know I'm going to be OK. Yeah, it's a party in the USA.
<v Luke Ma>给你。第二句：“举起双手。他们正在放我的歌。我知道我会没事的。是的，那是美国的一场派对。”

00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:13.580
<v Jiayang Fan>OK, great. That's more information. I'm going to have to go all the way for sentence number three. I think I'm going to need as much information as I can wrangle.
<v Jiayang Fan>好的，太棒了。那信息更多了。我得坚持到第三句。我想我需要尽可能多地获取信息。

00:11:13.580 --> 00:11:20.330
<v Luke Ma>All right. Sentence number three. Peter Piper picked a peck of pickled peppers.
<v Luke Ma>好的。第三句：“彼得·派珀（Peter Piper）摘了一啄腌辣椒。”

00:11:20.330 --> 00:11:27.030
<v Jiayang Fan>OK, so can I reach for my lifeline?
<v Jiayang Fan>好吧，我可以请求求助吗？

00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:40.470
<v Tobin Low>Yes. OK, so this is a secret lifeline that we are going to allow Jiayang to access, which is that Luke, we are going to ask you to say her name. That's all we're asking is just say her name.
<v Tobin Low>可以。这是我们要给贾扬的一个秘密求助机会，就是卢克，我们要请你说出她的名字。我们只需要你说她的名字。

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:41.220
<v Jiayang Fan>Right.
<v Jiayang Fan>好的。

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:44.730
<v Luke Ma>Sure. Fan Jiayang.
<v Luke Ma>当然。范贾扬。

00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:46.140
<v Jiayang Fan>Oof, OK.
<v Jiayang Fan>呜，好的。

00:11:46.140 --> 00:11:47.820
<v Tobin Low>Curveball, curveball.
<v Tobin Low>变化球，变化球。

00:11:47.820 --> 00:11:56.820
<v Jiayang Fan>I don't know if-- I don't know if that helped or confused, because clearly he is someone--
<v Jiayang Fan>我不知道这到底是帮了忙还是让我更困惑了，因为很显然他是一个……

00:11:56.820 --> 00:12:04.230
<v Tobin Low>Because you don't have an ABC like me just mangling your name left and right.
<v Tobin Low>因为你没有像我这样的ABC，名字被各种乱读。

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:30.960
<v Jiayang Fan>Because he obviously can speak Chinese, because he pronounced the syllables correctly, but also had a mastery of the tones. But I think I've been given all the information that is permissible. And I think I'm-- I have a number in my head.
