00:23:19.610 --> 00:23:20.120
<v Tobin Low>Yes.
<v Tobin Low>好的。

00:23:20.120 --> 00:23:38.230
<v Jiayang Fan>I'm going to alter my guess. And I will give you my explanation. I'm going to say-- I'm deciding between 15 and 16. I just can't quite pull the trigger. I'm going to go for 16.
<v Jiayang Fan>我打算改变猜测。我会给出我的解释。我……我在15和16之间犹豫。我下不了决心。我决定猜16岁。

00:23:38.230 --> 00:23:44.130
<v Tobin Low>OK, you're going for 16. You're coming up. Can you tell me a little bit why?
<v Tobin Low>好的，你猜16岁。你快到了。能告诉我为什么吗？

00:23:44.130 --> 00:24:04.500
<v Jiayang Fan>It's the way she says the word song. She kind of swallows the G in a way that I don't think a native ever would. So it came out like sung, rather than song. And when that happened, my ears perked up, because that's exactly the way that my mom would have said the word song.
<v Jiayang Fan>这是她说“歌”这个词的方式。她有点吞掉了G，以一种我觉得母语人士永远不会有的方式。听起来像“sung”，而不是“song”。当那发生时，我的耳朵竖了起来，因为那正是我妈妈说“song”这个词的方式。

00:24:04.500 --> 00:24:11.360
<v Tobin Low>OK, you can't see that I am squirming in my seat, because unfortunately you went the wrong direction.
<v Tobin Low>好的，你看不见我坐在座位上扭动，因为很遗憾，你猜反了方向。

00:24:11.360 --> 00:24:12.840
<v Jiayang Fan>No, no.
<v Jiayang Fan>不，不。

00:24:12.840 --> 00:24:21.680
<v Tobin Low>Your guess of 10 years old was closer, because the actual answer was that Larissa moved here when she was seven.
<v Tobin Low>你猜的10岁其实更接近，因为真实的答案是拉丽莎七岁时搬到这里的。

00:24:21.680 --> 00:24:24.190
<v Tobin Low>[HORN BLOWING]
<v Tobin Low>[喇叭响]

00:24:24.190 --> 00:24:27.690
<v Jiayang Fan>Oh, that's so frustrating, because that's the age I came here.
<v Jiayang Fan>噢，那太令人沮丧了，因为那正是我的年龄。

00:24:27.690 --> 00:24:33.060
<v Jiayang Fan>[LAUGHTER]
<v Jiayang Fan>[笑声]

00:24:33.060 --> 00:24:43.240
<v Jiayang Fan>And how aware are you of your accent? I mean, do you feel like you can hear it?
<v Jiayang Fan>你对自己的口音有多了解？我的意思是，你觉得你能听出来吗？

00:24:43.240 --> 00:25:15.010
<v Larissa Jo>I would say until that experience which was maybe seven, eight years ago with the recording book, I thought I was camouflaged. I thought I was good. And when I heard that they could tell I didn't sound like I was from the US, I felt-- I don't know, a little disappointed or a little offended. And then I spoke to my boyfriend at the time who is American, grew up in America.
<v Larissa Jo>我会说，直到七八年前那次录音书籍的经历，我以为我被“伪装”了。我以为我做得很好。当我听说他们能听出我听起来不像美国人时，我感到……我不知道，有点失望，或者有点被冒犯。后来我和我当时的男朋友谈过，他是美国人，在美国长大。

00:25:15.010 --> 00:25:36.940
<v Larissa Jo>And I said what, I don't have an accent. I speak really good English. And he said yes, you speak very good English. But you don't speak like an American. And I said, what do you mean? And he said for example, he slurs things. He doesn't enunciate. And then when he pointed this out, he said I enunciate everything.
<v Larissa Jo>我说什么？我没有口音。我英语说得很好。他说，是的，你的英语说得很好。但你说话不像美国人。我说，你什么意思？他说，例如，他说话会含糊不清。他不刻意发音。然后当他指出这一点时，他说我什么都发音得太清晰。

00:25:36.940 --> 00:25:42.220
<v Jiayang Fan>And when he said that, was that a moment of recognition? Or were you like, what are you talking about?
<v Jiayang Fan>当他那么说时，那是一个认知的瞬间吗？还是你觉得，你在说什么？

00:25:42.220 --> 00:25:55.000
<v Larissa Jo>Yeah. That really makes sense. I think it comes from making sure I am understood in English by my parents and other immigrants, wanting them to understand me.
<v Larissa Jo>是的。那真的很有道理。我认为这源于确保我能被我的父母和其他移民理解，我希望他们听懂我。

00:25:55.000 --> 00:26:08.630
<v Jiayang Fan>Right. And I wonder if there's a part of you that wants to have no accent at all. Like ideally, would it be better just to be completely accentless?
<v Jiayang Fan>对。我好奇你内心深处是否有一部分人希望完全没有口音？比如，如果可以的话，完全没有口音会更好吗？

00:26:08.630 --> 00:26:33.070
<v Larissa Jo>Yeah. You know, for a long time, my experiences had made me be ashamed of it. And now I'm just like, why? Why? I don't want that. I'm going to stand up for where I come from, the things that shaped me, the things that-- even the experiences that made me enunciate. Because this is something I did to help my community. And now it's really part of me.
<v Larissa Jo>是的。你知道，很长一段时间里，我的经历让我为此感到羞耻。而现在我只想说，为什么？为什么？我不想要那样。我要支持我的出身，塑造我的那些事物，那些……甚至让我过度发音的经历。因为这是我为了帮助社区所做的事情。而现在它真正成了我的一部分。

00:26:33.070 --> 00:27:28.150
<v Jiayang Fan>Yeah. No, I really like your logic there and also kind of the evolution of your comfort with how you speak. I mean, it's so interesting to talk to you, because I do feel like I'm talking to a version of myself that brings up all my anxieties about how I speak. But also, my aspiration for self acceptance. There's something about your acceptance of your accent that I find inspirational. It is not this race to be accentless, the way that I fear I have conceived of my journey in English. Like I do feel self-conscious about it.
<v Jiayang Fan>是的。不，我真的很喜欢你的逻辑，还有你对说话方式舒适度的演变。我的意思是，和你交谈非常有趣，因为我确实觉得自己在和一个版本的自己对话，这勾起了我所有关于说话方式的焦虑。但也勾起了我对自我接纳的渴望。你对口音的接纳让我想到了某种灵感。这不是那种想要完全没有口音的竞赛，就像我害怕的那样，我曾构思过我的英语旅程。我的意思是，我确实为此感到局促。

00:27:28.150 --> 00:27:37.000
<v Tobin Low>Larissa, if there was a piece of advice for Jiayang to get to where you are, is there something that helped you get to where you are?
<v Tobin Low>拉丽莎，如果给贾扬一个建议来达到你现在的境界，有什么东西能帮助你达到现在这个位置吗？

00:27:37.000 --> 00:27:41.210
<v Jiayang Fan>Oh, that's a great question.
<v Jiayang Fan>噢，那是个好问题。

00:27:41.210 --> 00:28:23.260
<v Larissa Jo>Yeah. So I guess maybe I would say-- so why do I have this accent? Why do I speak this way? And I say, well, it's because I lived in China for seven years. And then I say, well, if I don't want an accent, the way to not have it is to wish I hadn't lived in China. And to wish that I was maybe born in the States or something. And do I wish that? And I say, no, I don't wish that. So I want the experiences that also gave me this side effect of the way I speak. And they're an emblem, they're like a memory of that.
<v Larissa Jo>是的。我想也许我会说……我为什么有这个口音？我为什么这样说话？我会说，嗯，这是因为我在中国住了七年。然后我说，嗯，如果我不想要口音，不想要它的方式就是希望我没有在中国住过。希望我是在美国出生的什么的。我希望那样吗？我说，不，我不希望。所以我想要那些也给了我这种说话方式副作用的经历。它们是一个象征，它们就像那段记忆。

00:28:23.260 --> 00:28:48.390
<v Jiayang Fan>Thank you, Larissa. You are-- I feel like I'm meeting my future self, the person that I would-- the comfort with my accent that I am aspiring to be. So it's been a real pleasure to talk.
<v Jiayang Fan>谢谢你，拉丽莎。你……我觉得我遇见了未来的自己，那个我想要成为的……对自己的口音有着舒适感的自己。所以交谈真是太愉快了。

00:28:48.390 --> 00:29:13.080
<v Tobin Low>OK. So three contestants. I hate to add insult to injury. But I do have to say your score was zero for three. Maybe this is the time I should tell you, you will not be winning the brand new car. But also the car was going to be just a rental that we were going to give to you for the weekend.
<v Tobin Low>好的。所以三位参赛者。我不忍心伤口撒盐，但我不得不说你的比分是三投零中。也许现在我该告诉你，你赢不到那辆新车。而且那辆车本来也只是我们给你周末租的一辆车。

00:29:13.080 --> 00:29:20.760
<v Jiayang Fan>You've spared me from having to wrangle a friend into being my driver for the rental.
<v Jiayang Fan>你帮我省去了还得找个朋友来当租车司机的麻烦。

00:29:20.760 --> 00:29:37.990
<v Tobin Low>Well, Jiayang, thank you for playing our game. I would say see you next time, but I think we've learned that this game is impossible. And we would discourage it. No one should ever play this game ever again. Shut it down. Good night, audience. We'll see you not next time.
<v Tobin Low>嗯，贾扬，谢谢你玩我们的游戏。我会说下次见，但我想我们已经学到了这个游戏是不可能的。我们建议任何人再也别玩这个游戏了。结束吧。晚安，观众们。我们下次……不一定见。

00:29:37.990 --> 00:29:44.240
<v Jiayang Fan>Thank you, Tobin.
<v Jiayang Fan>谢谢你，托宾。

00:29:44.240 --> 00:29:59.290
<v Ira Glass>Tobin Low is one of the editors of our show. Coming up, can a simple game show save the world? We find out. That's in a minute. Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>托宾·洛是我们节目的编辑之一。接下来，简单的游戏节目能拯救世界吗？我们拭目以待。那是一分钟后的事情。芝加哥公共电台，节目继续。

00:29:59.290 --> 00:30:13.090
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life, I'm Ira Glass. Today's program, it's a game show. An entire program of stories presented not the way we usually do, but as game shows, radio game shows. We've arrived at Act Two of our show. And we're calling Act Two--
<v Ira Glass>这里是《This American Life》，我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目，这是一个游戏节目。一个充满故事的节目，不是以我们通常的方式，而是作为游戏节目，电台游戏节目呈现。我们来到了节目的第二幕。我们称之为……

00:30:13.090 --> 00:30:16.270
<v George Gray>You Bet Your Planet's Life.
<v George Gray>“拿你的行星生命打赌”。

00:30:16.270 --> 00:30:44.020
<v Ira Glass>That's George Gray, who agreed to take a little time out from his day job as the announcer of The Price Is Right to help us with this next game. And before I get to it, I should explain that this one comes from a question that I have had myself about this thing that I've seen in the news. You know the Paris Agreement on climate change and how it has these goals that countries are supposed to meet if we're going to hold the planet to less than two degrees of warming overall and ideally just 1.5 degrees of warming?
<v Ira Glass>那是乔治·格雷（George Gray），他同意从他的全职工作——《价格猜猜看》（The Price Is Right）播音员——中抽空出来帮助我们完成下一个游戏。在我开始之前，我应该解释一下，这源于我自己对新闻中看到的一件事的疑问。你知道《巴黎协定》关于气候变化的规定，以及它设定的目标，如果我们想把全球变暖控制在两度以内，理想情况下仅在1.5度以内，各国必须实现这些目标？

00:30:44.020 --> 00:31:02.770
<v Ira Glass>And I've wondered for a while now. Is there any chance at all, any chance, that the United States is going to make the goals? Like forget about the rest of the world for a minute. Just talking about the part of this that we in this country control. Will we as a country, one of the biggest carbon emitters, will we make our goal?
<v Ira Glass>我有一段时间都在想：美国有一丁点可能实现这些目标吗？忘记世界其他地方，只谈我们国家能控制的那部分。我们国家，作为最大的碳排放国之一，会实现我们的目标吗？

00:31:02.770 --> 00:31:26.030
<v Ira Glass>To do that, we would have to cut our emissions by 50% by the year 2030 for starters. That's just eight years from now. Is there any kind of realistic path that gets us there? Well, today's game show episode seemed like a perfect opportunity to finally find out. And so with that in mind, we turn to our next game show.
<v Ira Glass>要做到这一点，我们首先必须在2030年之前将排放量削减50%。距离现在只有八年了。有什么现实的路径能让我们达到那里吗？好吧，今天的游戏节目似乎是一个完美的机会来最终找出答案。带着这个想法，我们转向下一个游戏节目。

00:31:26.030 --> 00:31:48.060
<v George Gray>Welcome to You Bet Your Planet's Life. Is there any chance at all, ladies and gentlemen, that we will actually make our goals and cut our emissions to half? Well, stay tuned, because today we find out and win valuable prizes.
<v George Gray>欢迎来到“拿你的行星生命打赌”。女士们、先生们，我们真的有可能实现目标并将排放量减半吗？敬请收听，因为今天我们要揭晓答案，并赢得宝贵奖品。

00:31:48.060 --> 00:31:52.170
<v Ira Glass>Well, thank you, George Gray, for that nice intro. And why don't we bring on today's contestant?
<v Ira Glass>好吧，感谢乔治·格雷带来的精彩开场。为什么不请出今天的参赛者呢？

00:31:52.170 --> 00:32:18.990
<v George Gray>Well, Ira, hailing from New York City and Austin, Texas, director of research at Columbia University's Center on Global Energy Policy, she's modeled parts of the climate and energy future and worked around the globe for the US government, the International Energy Agency and the Asia-Pacific Energy Research Center. Say that three times fast. Please welcome Melissa Lott. Come on down.
<v George Gray>好吧，艾拉，她来自纽约市和得克萨斯州奥斯汀，是哥伦比亚大学全球能源政策中心的研究主任。她模拟过气候和能源的未来，并为美国政府、国际能源署和亚太能源研究中心在世界各地工作过。把它快速说三遍。欢迎梅丽莎·洛特（Melissa Lott）。快请进。

00:32:18.990 --> 00:32:20.820
<v Ira Glass>Well, welcome, Melissa. And I hope you're ready to play.
<v Ira Glass>欢迎，梅丽莎。希望你准备好开始游戏了。

00:32:20.820 --> 00:32:22.530
<v Melissa Lott>I'm excited, Ira. Ready to go.
<v Melissa Lott>我很兴奋，艾拉。准备好了。

00:32:22.530 --> 00:32:30.930
<v Ira Glass>Now I understand that you are a super taster. Was there a moment where you discovered that?
<v Ira Glass>据我了解你是一位超级品鉴师。有什么瞬间让你发现这一点吗？

00:32:30.930 --> 00:32:50.070
<v Melissa Lott>There was a moment. I was eating a piece of chocolate. And I went to my friend, I think this one's from Ecuador. And they went, how on earth do you know that? And I said, well, it's so different than the other one. They're like, no, it's not. They don't taste different. And I was like, they are completely different. And they ran off and got a bunch of different ones for me to try. It turns out I can tell the difference blindfolded.
<v Melissa Lott>有一个瞬间。我在吃一块巧克力。我对我朋友说，我想这块是厄瓜多尔的。他们说，你到底是怎么知道的？我说，嗯，它和其他的一点都不一样。他们说，不，没区别。它们尝起来一样。我说，它们完全不同。他们跑去拿了一堆不同的给我试。结果证明我可以蒙着眼辨别出区别。

00:32:50.070 --> 00:32:53.010
<v Ira Glass>Well, those are skills that will not aid you in our game today.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，那些技能在今天的游戏中可帮不了你。

00:32:53.010 --> 00:32:54.070
<v Melissa Lott>Probably not.
<v Melissa Lott>大概不会。

00:32:54.070 --> 00:33:13.140
<v Ira Glass>And let me ask you to take a look up there in the big board, because in this first round, here's what we're going to do. We're going to try to get to 50% cuts in emissions by the year 2030. Or to be very precise about this, our country's goal for the Paris Agreement is to cut 50 to 52% of where our emissions were back in the year 2005.
<v Ira Glass>请看看大屏幕，因为在第一轮中，我们要这样做：我们要尝试在2030年之前将排放量削减50%。或者更精确地说，我们国家为《巴黎协定》设定的目标是比2005年的排放水平削减50%到52%。

00:33:13.140 --> 00:33:28.920
<v Ira Glass>And Melissa, in each of your turns that we're going to do, you will propose a number for the amount of emissions that you think we can cut. And then you'll explain how you think we can cut it. If the judges rule that that's credible, if there's reasonable evidence that your proposal could work at the scale and speed you say it could, you will hear this sound.
<v Ira Glass>梅丽莎，在你进行的每一回合中，你都会提出一个你认为我们可以削减的排放量数字。然后解释你认为我们怎么做才能削减它。如果评委裁定那是可信的，如果证据表明你的提议能在你说得出的规模和速度下起作用，你会听到这个声音。

00:33:28.920 --> 00:33:29.340
<v Ira Glass>[DING]
<v Ira Glass>[叮]

00:33:29.340 --> 00:33:34.940
<v Ira Glass>And it goes up on the board. If the judges think your plan is bogus, malarkey, impossible, you will hear this sound.
<v Ira Glass>它会出现在板子上。如果评委认为你的计划是假的、胡闹的、不可能的，你会听到这个声音。

00:33:34.940 --> 00:33:35.700
<v Ira Glass>[BUZZ]
<v Ira Glass>[嗡]

00:33:35.700 --> 00:33:39.210
<v Ira Glass>And we all take one step closer to planetary disaster. No pressure.
<v Ira Glass>然后我们大家都向行星灾难迈进一步。没压力吧。

00:33:39.210 --> 00:33:40.950
<v Melissa Lott>No pressure at all.
<v Melissa Lott>一点压力都没有。

00:33:40.950 --> 00:33:43.870
<v Ira Glass>And George, where do we start?
<v Ira Glass>乔治，我们从哪儿开始？

00:33:43.870 --> 00:33:50.850
<v George Gray>Well, Ira, Melissa's starting our game with 25% already on the board.
<v George Gray>嗯，艾拉，梅丽莎已经以25%的基数开启了我们的游戏。

00:33:50.850 --> 00:33:58.140
<v Ira Glass>So George, maybe you should explain exactly what that's about. Because this surprised me. I really didn't know this before we all started working on this game show.
<v Ira Glass>乔治，也许你应该解释一下那到底是怎么回事。因为这让我很惊讶。在我们开始制作这个游戏节目之前，我真的不知道这一点。

00:33:58.140 --> 00:34:32.730
<v George Gray>I'd love to, Ira. Since 2005, we've already reduced our emissions around 18%. That's because of cheap wind power, cheap solar power, cheap natural gas replacing coal. And because states, local communities, and big corporations are making moves to lower their emissions. But wait, there's more. Another 7% or so is locked in and ready to roll by the end of the decade. All told, 18% plus 7% gives you-- carry the 1, move it over to 2-- 25 big percentage points.
<v George Gray>我很乐意，艾拉。自2005年以来，我们已经减少了约18%的排放量。那是因为廉价的风能、太阳能、廉价的天然气替代了煤炭。而且是因为各州、地方社区和大型企业正在采取行动降低排放。但还有更多，在十年结束前，另外7%左右已经锁定并准备好实施。总而言之，18%加上7%，得到……进位1，移动到2……25个大百分点。

00:34:32.730 --> 00:34:45.390
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, that was news to me. Our emissions have been going down ever since 2007 in this country. But what that means is we are already halfway to our goal of 50% by 2030 before we even start our game.
<v Ira Glass>是的，这对我也算新闻。自2007年以来，我国的排放量一直在下降。但这意味着在我们开始游戏之前，我们已经完成了2030年减排50%目标的一半。

00:34:45.390 --> 00:34:47.190
<v George Gray>That's right, Ira.
<v George Gray>那是正确的，艾拉。

00:34:47.190 --> 00:34:58.880
<v Ira Glass>So Melissa, your goal is to credibly get us the next 25% on top of that in just eight years. And let's just jump in. How much do you want to put on the board for your first turn?
<v Ira Glass>所以梅丽莎，你的目标是在短短八年内，在此基础上再可信地获得25%。我们直接开始吧。第一回合你想在板子上放多少？

00:34:58.880 --> 00:35:01.910
<v Melissa Lott>So I'm going to go for another 15%.
<v Melissa Lott>我要再增加15%。

00:35:01.910 --> 00:35:05.000
<v Ira Glass>OK, 15%. And how are you going to get that 15%?
<v Ira Glass>好的，15%。你打算如何实现那15%？

00:35:05.000 --> 00:35:11.000
<v Melissa Lott>So I think that President Biden's big climate and energy law is going to actually get us all the way to that 15%.
<v Melissa Lott>我认为拜登总统的气候和能源法案实际上将使我们达到那15%。

00:35:11.000 --> 00:35:12.740
<v Ira Glass>Is that true? It'll get us that much?
<v Ira Glass>是真的吗？能达到那么多？

00:35:12.740 --> 00:35:19.220
<v Melissa Lott>Yeah, so it's called the Inflation Reduction Act. But really, it's the biggest energy and climate legislation we've ever passed. It's massive.
<v Melissa Lott>是的，它被称为《通胀削减法案》。但实际上，这是我们有史以来通过的最大规模的能源和气候立法。它是巨大的。

00:35:19.220 --> 00:35:20.840
<v Ira Glass>And so how does it do that?
<v Ira Glass>那么它是如何做到的？

00:35:20.840 --> 00:35:46.280
<v Melissa Lott>So it's a whole host of different things. It's tax credits supporting clean electricity. So I'm talking about solar and wind and nuclear, all the stuff we need to produce zero carbon electricity. And then it gives us a lot of tax credits to bring a lot of electric vehicles on the road. There's tax credits for businesses to help them insulate their buildings, let's say, so they can use less energy in the first place. And there's tax credits for capturing carbon before it actually goes into the atmosphere when we're making stuff like concrete and cement.
<v Melissa Lott>它是一系列不同的举措。它是支持清洁电力的税收抵免。所以我说的是太阳能、风能和核能，所有我们需要生产零碳电力的东西。然后它给了我们大量的税收抵免，以使大量的电动汽车上路。还有给企业的税收抵免，帮助他们给建筑物隔热，这样他们就能首先使用更少的能源。还有在制造混凝土和水泥等产品时，在碳排放到大气之前进行捕获的税收抵免。

00:35:46.280 --> 00:35:48.980
<v Ira Glass>Wait, is concrete and cement actually kind of a big deal?
<v Ira Glass>等等，混凝土和水泥真的有那么重要吗？

00:35:48.980 --> 00:35:51.770
<v Melissa Lott>Yeah, it's huge. It's really huge.
<v Melissa Lott>是的，非常巨大。真的非常大。

00:35:51.770 --> 00:35:59.480
<v Ira Glass>OK, so you're saying that all these things, they'll add up. And these things together get us to 15% of our goals under the Paris Agreement.
<v Ira Glass>好的，所以你说所有这些东西加起来，在《巴黎协定》下加起来就是我们目标的15%。

00:35:59.480 --> 00:36:05.210
<v Melissa Lott>So some people are saying 10%. Some are saying 20%. I'm good with 15%, right in the middle.
<v Melissa Lott>有些人说是10%。有些人说是20%。我选15%，正好在中间。

00:36:05.210 --> 00:36:08.480
<v Ira Glass>All right. Let's turn to our judges and see what they say.
<v Ira Glass>好的。看看我们的评委怎么说。

00:36:08.480 --> 00:36:13.310
<v George Gray>Oh, Ira, the judges say yes.
<v George Gray>噢，艾拉，评委们说yes。

00:36:13.310 --> 00:36:15.430
<v George Gray>[DING]
<v George Gray>[叮]

00:36:15.430 --> 00:36:17.470
<v Ira Glass>So George, what's Melissa's score?
<v Ira Glass>乔治，梅丽莎的得分是多少？

00:36:17.470 --> 00:36:30.140
<v George Gray>Well, Ira, the big board is at 40%, which means she needs another 10% to reach our goal of 50% emissions cut.
<v George Gray>嗯，艾拉，大屏幕现在是40%，这意味着她还需要10%才能达到我们50%的减排目标。

00:36:30.140 --> 00:36:38.690
<v Ira Glass>OK, so now it is your second turn, Melissa. In your second turn, how much do you want to put on the board?
<v Ira Glass>好的，现在是你的第二回合，梅丽莎。第二回合你想在板子上放多少？

00:36:38.690 --> 00:36:44.870
<v Melissa Lott>I think I'm going to go for it and put up a whole 10%. I'm going to go all the way to 50%.
<v Melissa Lott>我想我会全力以赴，放上整整10%。我打算一路达到50%。

00:36:44.870 --> 00:36:51.260
<v Ira Glass>OK, all right. Going all the way. Daring. What do you got?
<v Ira Glass>好的，好的。全力以赴。大胆。你有什么打算？

00:36:51.260 --> 00:37:08.960
<v Melissa Lott>OK, so I should say right off the bat that what I have is a whole hodgepodge of stuff. So it's a lot of different things. And so what I'm looking at doing is saying where do we already have momentum? Where are we already moving and we just need to move a little bit faster? So squeeze a couple more percentage points out of the things that are already working for us.
<v Melissa Lott>好的，首先我要说，我手头是一堆各种各样的东西。所以有很多不同的因素。所以我所做的是观察我们哪里已经有了动力？我们在哪里已经行动起来，只需要行动得更快一点？从已经对我们有效的措施中再挤出几个百分点。

00:37:08.960 --> 00:37:10.190
<v Ira Glass>OK, which means what?
<v Ira Glass>好的，这意味着什么？

00:37:10.190 --> 00:37:23.150
<v Melissa Lott>So this means three buckets to me. So the first one is electricity. So it's doing all the stuff that's in the Inflation Reduction Act, but pushing it further. Building more solar, more wind, more nuclear, storing more electricity.
<v Melissa Lott>这意味着对我来说有三个领域。第一个是电力。所以做所有《通胀削减法案》里提到的事情，但更进一步。建设更多的太阳能、风能、核能，储存更多的电力。

00:37:23.150 --> 00:37:26.030
<v Ira Glass>OK, bucket two.
<v Ira Glass>好的，领域二。

00:37:26.030 --> 00:37:35.600
<v Melissa Lott>So bucket two is about working with just a few big industries. So the ones that produce a lot of greenhouse gases. How do we work with them to get their emissions down really quickly?
<v Melissa Lott>领域二关于与几个大行业合作。就是那些产生大量温室气体的行业。我们如何与他们合作以非常快速地降低排放？

00:37:35.600 --> 00:37:37.010
<v Ira Glass>So for example?
<v Ira Glass>例如？

00:37:37.010 --> 00:37:43.580
<v Melissa Lott>Steel's a good example. We make steel with coal today, but we can make it with electricity. So let's do that.
<v Melissa Lott>钢铁是个好例子。我们今天用煤炭制造钢铁，但我们可以用电力制造。所以我们这么做。

00:37:43.580 --> 00:37:45.140
<v Ira Glass>OK, bucket three.
<v Ira Glass>好的，领域三。

00:37:45.140 --> 00:38:05.630
<v Melissa Lott>So bucket three, I'm thinking about waste. So there's two big things that I want us to stop wasting and that would get us a few more percentage points. So the first part of this is about reducing greenhouse gas emissions that we just are throwing in the atmosphere. We're just wasting them. The big one here is methane. So it's fixing leaky pipes. It's about plugging abandoned oil and gas wells, stopping putting methane into the atmosphere.
<v Melissa Lott>领域三，我考虑的是浪费。有两件大事我想让我们停止浪费，那样就能让我们多获得几个百分点。第一部分关于减少我们仅仅扔进大气的温室气体排放。我们只是在浪费它们。最大的一个是甲烷。所以是修复泄漏的管道。是堵塞废弃的油气井，停止将甲烷排放到大气中。

00:38:05.630 --> 00:38:07.070
<v Ira Glass>And the second kind?
<v Ira Glass>第二种浪费？

00:38:07.070 --> 00:38:22.430
<v Melissa Lott>The second kind of waste is the waste we have when it comes to zero carbon electricity. So we've already built a lot of wind turbines, a lot of nuclear power plants, a lot of solar panels. And we're not using all that at zero carbon electricity. We're just wasting a lot of it.
<v Melissa Lott>第二种浪费是我们拥有零碳电力时的浪费。我们已经建造了大量的风力涡轮机、核电站、太阳能电池板。而我们并没有使用所有的零碳电力。我们只是浪费了其中的很大一部分。

00:38:22.430 --> 00:38:24.110
<v Ira Glass>Like why?
<v Ira Glass>为什么？

00:38:24.110 --> 00:38:29.670
<v Melissa Lott>There's a bunch of different reasons. But it comes down to supply and demand not matching up.
<v Melissa Lott>有很多不同的原因。但归根结底是供需不匹配。

00:38:29.670 --> 00:38:38.570
<v Ira Glass>Is this a thing of like-- the solar energy gets made, but there's no way to store it and there's no way to get it to the homes where it's needed? Is that what you mean?
<v Ira Glass>这是像……太阳能制造出来了，但没有储存方式，也没有办法把它传送到需要的家庭里吗？你是这个意思吗？

00:38:38.570 --> 00:38:46.340
<v Melissa Lott>Yeah. And that's like-- it's such a waste, Ira. It's like, we already paid for it. It's already there. And we're not using it.
<v Melissa Lott>是的。那真是……太浪费了，艾拉。就像，我们已经付过钱了。它已经在那里了。而我们没在使用它。

00:38:46.340 --> 00:38:48.680
<v Ira Glass>And so to fix that, what do we do?
<v Ira Glass>那么为了解决那个问题，我们该做什么？

00:38:48.680 --> 00:38:55.280
<v Melissa Lott>So to fix that, we build a lot of wires to move the stuff around. And then we also figure out ways to store that electricity.
<v Melissa Lott>所以为了解决它，我们建造大量的电线来输送电力。然后我们也寻找储存电力的办法。

00:38:55.280 --> 00:39:11.400
<v Ira Glass>OK, so between those three buckets, you're saying that adds up to 10%. Can I ask you in the most real way possible, do you think that's actually going to happen? Are we actually going to get to 50% by 2030?
<v Ira Glass>好的，在这三个领域之间，你说这加起来是10%。我能以最现实的方式问你吗？你认为那真的会发生吗？我们真的会在2030年之前达到50%吗？

00:39:11.400 --> 00:39:26.930
<v Melissa Lott>I think we're going to get close. I don't know if we're going to get all the way there. I mean, we could overshoot it, honestly. There's a lot of different factors that go into that, including behavior, including broader things that are happening in the world that I have no insight into.
<v Melissa Lott>我认为我们会接近。我不知道我们是否能一路走到那里。我的意思是，老实说，我们甚至可能超额完成。有很多不同的因素会导致这一点，包括行为，包括世界上正在发生且我无法洞察的更广泛的事情。

00:39:26.930 --> 00:39:45.190
<v Melissa Lott>What's going to keep us from maybe getting there as fast as we want is all the non-technical stuff. So being able to actually build something, get it permitted, get it paid for in our markets. I think it's going to be close. I don't know if we're going to get over the 50% line in time, though.
<v Melissa Lott>可能阻碍我们像我们希望的那样快速到达那里的，是所有非技术性的东西。所以能够真正建造某样东西，获得许可，在我们的市场上获得资助。我认为会很接近。但我不知道我们是否能及时跨过50%的线。

00:39:45.190 --> 00:39:48.370
<v Ira Glass>All right. Well, judges, what do you think?
<v Ira Glass>好的。好吧，评委，你们怎么看？

00:39:48.370 --> 00:39:56.550
<v George Gray>Oh, wait. Wait, is this coming in? The judges say yes.
<v George Gray>噢，等等。等等，这进来了吗？评委说yes。

00:39:56.550 --> 00:39:59.850
<v Ira Glass>Well, congratulations, Melissa, for winning round one.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，恭喜你，梅丽莎，赢得了第一轮。

00:39:59.850 --> 00:40:06.630
<v Melissa Lott>Thank you, Ira. Thank you, George. I appreciate it. But remember, this is only round one. There's another round after this.
<v Melissa Lott>谢谢你，艾拉。谢谢，乔治。我很感激。但请记住，这只是第一轮。后面还有一轮。

00:40:06.630 --> 00:40:30.880
<v Ira Glass>Well, exactly. That's exactly what I was going to say. You progressed to our bonus round. Let's talk about that next round, because the first goal that America set for itself with the Paris Agreement is to cut 50% of our emissions by 2030. But the bigger goal is to cut all greenhouse gases as far as possible to get to net zero by 2050. In your view, is there a credible path towards that that you can imagine actually happening?
<v Ira Glass>嗯，完全正确。那正是我要说的。你晋级了我们的奖金轮。让我们谈谈那一轮，因为美国在《巴黎协定》中为自己设定的第一个目标是在2030年削减50%的排放量。但更大的目标是尽可能多地削减所有温室气体，到2050年实现净零排放。在你看来，你能想象出一条通往那里的可信路径吗？

00:40:30.880 --> 00:40:45.560
<v Melissa Lott>There's definitely a credible path. I think there is. But like with the next eight years, it's going to be challenging and it requires us to make a choice to get on that path. And a lot of it is just doing a lot more of what we've already been talking about.
<v Melissa Lott>绝对有一条可信的路径。我认为有。但就像接下来的八年一样，这将会充满挑战，需要我们做出选择才能踏上那条路。而且很大程度上只是做更多我们已经谈过的事情。

00:40:45.560 --> 00:41:01.680
<v Melissa Lott>Not just making more buildings more energy efficient. We're making every single building more energy efficient. We're electrifying those buildings. We're pulling all the emissions out of those buildings. We're getting every single car and truck and bus and plane to zero emissions. Every single thing in our transportation.
<v Melissa Lott>不仅仅是让更多的建筑提高能源效率。我们要让每一栋建筑都提高能效。我们要使这些建筑实现电气化。我们要把这些建筑里的所有排放物清理掉。我们要让每一辆汽车、卡车、公交车和飞机实现零排放。我们在运输中的每一件事。

00:41:01.680 --> 00:41:08.840
<v Ira Glass>When you say it this way, it just seems so big. It's hard to fathom that that could actually happen.
<v Ira Glass>当你这样说时，它看起来太大了。很难想象那真的会发生。

00:41:08.840 --> 00:41:25.120
<v Melissa Lott>I mean, it's massive. It is massive. It is a huge, huge undertaking. Some people describe it as like a wartime effort. I think that's right. Like it's massive to do this over the next just few decades. We're talking about 2050. That's not that long from now.
<v Melissa Lott>我的意思是，它是巨大的。它是巨大的。这是一项巨大、巨大的事业。有些人把它描述为一场战时努力。我认为那是对的。就像在接下来的几十年里做这件事是巨大的。我们谈论的是2050年。那距离现在并不长。

00:41:25.120 --> 00:41:28.820
<v Ira Glass>Well, that makes me feel not so super hopeful, just to say.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，这让我感觉不那么超级有希望了，只能说。

00:41:28.820 --> 00:41:59.910
<v Melissa Lott>But if we don't actually get there, we know the costs of inaction, of not moving there. And they're not pretty. So I'm talking about extreme events that flood Houston, or you know, swing across Florida. Heat waves in Southern California that lead to people dying. When you start to add up all these health costs and also the insurance costs, the cost of rebuilding homes, it's so much larger than building solar panels and nuclear power plants and retrofitting buildings and replacing cars.
<v Melissa Lott>但如果我们真的没能达到那里，我们知道不作为、不行动的代价。它们可不好看。所以我谈论的是淹没休斯顿的极端事件，或者横扫佛罗里达的灾难。南加州导致人员死亡的热浪。当你开始把所有这些健康成本，还有保险成本、重建房屋的成本加起来时，它比建造太阳能电池板、核电站、改造建筑和更换汽车要大得多。

00:41:59.910 --> 00:42:10.800
<v Ira Glass>Well, listen, thank you so much for playing our game. Let's bring in some inappropriately cheerful game show music. And George, tell her what she's won.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，听着，非常感谢你参与我们的游戏。让我们来点不那么合适的游戏节目音乐。乔治，告诉她赢了什么。

00:42:10.800 --> 00:42:27.600
<v George Gray>Well, Melissa, how about a brand new SUV? Ooh. Two plane tickets to-- ugh. A subscription to Diesel and Steak Lovers magazine.
<v George Gray>好吧，梅丽莎，一辆崭新的SUV怎么样？喔。两张飞往……呃。订阅《柴油和牛排爱好者》杂志。

00:42:27.600 --> 00:42:29.010
<v Ira Glass>What else you got there, buddy?
<v Ira Glass>你还有什么别的，伙计？

00:42:29.010 --> 00:42:36.870
<v George Gray>Well, Melissa, this is perfect. How about autographed copies of the collected works of Bill McKibben?
<v George Gray>好吧，梅丽莎，这太完美了。比尔·麦基本（Bill McKibben）全集的亲笔签名版怎么样？

00:42:36.870 --> 00:42:45.140
<v Ira Glass>That's more like it. Well, listen, thanks again, Melissa. And George, take it away.
<v Ira Glass>那更像样了。好吧，听着，再次感谢你，梅丽莎。乔治，交给你了。

00:42:45.140 --> 00:43:00.940
<v George Gray>Thanks so much, everybody. And we'll see you next time, if we're not all fleeing a wildfire or hurricane on You Bet Your Planet's Life.
<v George Gray>非常感谢大家。如果不是我们都在逃离野火或飓风，我们下次见，就在“拿你的行星生命打赌”节目。

00:43:00.940 --> 00:43:19.960
<v Ira Glass>Act 3-- You Didn't Hear It From Me. No, really, you didn't. This last game actually happened on another show, a podcast called Normal Gossip. And if you haven't heard Normal Gossip, the way it works, people in the show basically dish about actual gossip. Like true stories about normal, everyday people sent in by listeners.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕——你没听我说过。不，真的，你没听过。这最后一个游戏实际上发生在另一个节目里，一个叫《Normal Gossip》的播客。如果你没听过《Normal Gossip》，它是这样运作的：节目中的人基本上会吐槽真正的八卦。就像听众寄来的关于普通人的真实故事。

00:43:19.960 --> 00:43:41.860
<v Ira Glass>And last season, the producers recreated a version of-- you know that old game, telephone, where somebody whispers something to the person next to them who whispers it to the person next to them and on down the line. One of our producers, Sean Cole, has this rundown of what happened when they did a version of that on Normal Gossip and how it revealed some interesting things about the stories that we tell each other. Here's Sean.
<v Ira Glass>上一季，制作者重现了一个版本……你知道那个老游戏，传话游戏（telephone），某人对旁边的人低声说些什么，那个人再对旁边的人低声说，就这样一直传下去。我们的制作人之一，肖恩·科尔（Sean Cole），对他们在《Normal Gossip》做那个版本时发生的事情做了总结，它揭示了一些关于我们互相讲述的故事的有趣真相。这是肖恩。

00:43:41.860 --> 00:43:55.210
<v Sean Cole>The folks at Normal Gossip, they created the game for a very practical reason. While they were gearing up for season two, they got to talking about how long it had taken during season one for the guests to relax and settle into the conversation.
<v Sean Cole>《Normal Gossip》的人们出于一个非常实际的原因创造了这个游戏。当他们为第二季做准备时，他们开始谈论第一季嘉宾放松并进入谈话状态需要多长时间。

00:43:55.210 --> 00:44:04.090
<v Alex Sujong Laughlin>And so we were having this brainstorming session where we were talking about ways that we could just give them some more time and space ahead of the recording to loosen up.
<v Alex Sujong Laughlin>所以我们在开头脑风暴会议时，讨论如何能在录音前给他们更多的时间和空间来放松。

00:44:04.090 --> 00:44:07.480
<v Sean Cole>This is the producer of Normal Gossip, Alex Sujong Laughlin.
<v Sean Cole>这是《Normal Gossip》的制作人，亚历克斯·苏琼·劳克林（Alex Sujong Laughlin）。

00:44:07.480 --> 00:44:15.370
<v Alex Sujong Laughlin>And I came up with this idea that what if we had them play a game that is a game of gossip?
<v Alex Sujong Laughlin>我提出了一个想法，如果我们让他们玩一个关于八卦的游戏会怎样？

00:44:15.370 --> 00:44:34.930
<v Sean Cole>Gossip itself, of course, is sort of like a game of telephone. So they figured, let's just do that. Alex grabbed one of the gossip stories from their inbox and read it to the first guest that they had. And then recorded her telling it right back, just whatever that person could remember, played that for the second guest, had them tell it back and so on.
<v Sean Cole>八卦本身，当然，有点像传话游戏。所以他们想，就这么办。亚历克斯从他们的收件箱里抓取了一个八卦故事，读给他们请来的第一位嘉宾听。然后录下她直接复述出来，就是那个人能记住的任何内容，然后放给第二位嘉宾听，让他们复述，以此类推。

00:44:34.930 --> 00:44:42.460
<v Alex Sujong Laughlin>I told them not to take notes, because you don't generally take notes when somebody's telling you a gossip story, right? You're not like--
<v Alex Sujong Laughlin>我告诉他们不要做笔记，因为当有人给你讲八卦故事时，你通常不会做笔记，对吧？你不会像……

00:44:42.460 --> 00:44:44.560
<v Sean Cole>Unless you're a gossip columnist, I suppose.
<v Sean Cole>除非你是八卦专栏作家，我猜。

00:44:44.560 --> 00:44:58.630
<v Alex Sujong Laughlin>Yeah, exactly. Unless you're me. So yeah, I said do the best you can. If you don't remember something, that's fine. If you feel like tweaking a detail for fun, you can.
<v Alex Sujong Laughlin>是的，完全正确。除非你是像我这样的人。所以是的，我说尽力而为。如果你不记得什么，没关系。如果你觉得为了好玩想要调整一下细节，也可以。

00:44:58.630 --> 00:45:17.290
<v Sean Cole>But for the most part, try and be pretty faithful to the story you heard. Alex didn't really expect anything interesting to happen. But then when she listened through to all the versions, she realized she was seeing in real time how gossip works, how a story can evolve and change. That there was a logic to how it changed.
<v Sean Cole>但大部分时候，尽量忠实于你听到的故事。亚历克斯其实没期望发生任何有趣的事。但当她听完所有版本时，她意识到她在实时观察八卦是如何运作的，一个故事如何演变和改变。关于它如何改变，是有逻辑的。

00:45:17.290 --> 00:45:33.550
<v Sean Cole>Sure, there was some willy-nilly embellishing here and there. But for the most part, the changes were for a very specific reason. And I'm going to get to that. But first, you need to hear the original version of the story, so that you can appreciate when we get there how different it was by the end of the game.
<v Sean Cole>当然，到处都有随意的修饰。但大部分时候，改变是有非常具体的原因的。我要说到这一点。但首先，你需要听到故事的原始版本，这样当你听到最后时，你就能领会它是多么不同。

00:45:33.550 --> 00:45:36.550
<v Alex Sujong Laughlin>Yes, I have the text here.
<v Alex Sujong Laughlin>是的，我有文本在这儿。

00:45:36.550 --> 00:45:52.570
<v Sean Cole>I asked Alex to read it to me. I should say with every gossip story they tell on the show, the names and all the identifying details are changed. So it's completely anonymized. This story concerns two people who they called Kyle and Elliot. They started dating sometime before the pandemic.
