<v Tobin Low>他其实是一个非常虔诚、保守的人。所以他在欺骗这些男人进入和他那种关系。那全是个骗局。全是个骗局。然后他谈到我最近对一个家伙做了这种事。我们一起养了只猫，米斯托菲里斯先生。然后我掏空了他所有的钱。而且实际上，那是一段非常糟糕、有毒的关系。

00:55:35.620 --> 00:55:47.590
<v Sean Cole>Did you hear what happened there? Basically, Tobin took these loose facts from Bobby and Lindsey's telling-- the empty apartment, Kyle being straight, and very religious and conservative-- and he combines all those things.
<v Sean Cole>你听到发生了什么吗？基本上，托宾把鲍比和林赛讲述的那些松散的事实——空荡荡的公寓、凯尔是直的、而且非常虔诚保守——结合了所有那些东西。

00:55:47.590 --> 00:55:55.030
<v Alex Sujong Laughlin>Into a catfishing scam artist, who is not only doing that, but bragging about it on TikTok.
<v Alex Sujong Laughlin>变成了一个骗子，不仅如此，还在TikTok上炫耀。

00:55:55.030 --> 00:55:57.400
<v Sean Cole>This is Alex again, the Normal Gossip producer.
<v Sean Cole>这是亚历克斯，《Normal Gossip》的制作人。

00:55:57.400 --> 00:56:11.200
<v Alex Sujong Laughlin>Which seems to me like-- honestly, this is the turn that makes the least logical sense to me. Sorry, Tobin. But like if he's a scam artist, why would you be bragging about it? Then your scam is ruined. You know?
<v Alex Sujong Laughlin>这对我来说……老实说，这是逻辑上最不通的转弯。抱歉，托宾。但如果他是骗子，为什么你要炫耀？那你的骗局不就毁了吗？你知道吗？

00:56:11.200 --> 00:56:12.460
<v Sean Cole>I hadn't even thought of that.
<v Sean Cole>我甚至没想过那个。

00:56:12.460 --> 00:56:19.370
<v Alex Sujong Laughlin>And it's funny, because Tobin has said multiple times, he thought he was telling it perfectly.
<v Alex Sujong Laughlin>很有趣，因为托宾多次说过，他以为他讲得非常完美。

00:56:19.370 --> 00:56:25.390
<v Tobin Low>I stand by what I said. I thought that I told it exactly as it was told to me.
<v Tobin Low>我坚持我说的。我以为我讲得和我听到的完全一样。

00:56:25.390 --> 00:56:30.760
<v Sean Cole>This, of course, is my colleague Tobin Low. He deigned to consent to an interview for this story.
<v Sean Cole>这，当然，是我的同事托宾·洛。他屈尊同意接受这个故事的采访。

00:56:30.760 --> 00:56:40.140
<v Tobin Low>And if you had asked me afterwards, the only liberty I was very conscious of taking was changing the cat's name, which I thought was just fun and harmless.
<v Tobin Low>如果你事后问我，我唯一有意识采取的自由就是改了猫的名字，我觉得那只是有趣且无害的。

00:56:40.140 --> 00:56:56.760
<v Sean Cole>To be fair, Bobby did use the word scam one time in his and Lindsey's telling of the story. But there was nothing about catfishing. And certainly no mention of multiple victims of same. Tobin ultimately heard the original version of the story. And I had to wonder what he thought about it.
<v Sean Cole>公平地说，鲍比在他和林赛的故事讲述中确实用过一次“诈骗”这个词。但没有任何关于钓鱼欺诈（catfishing）的内容。更不用说多个受害者。托宾最终听到了故事的原始版本。我不禁想知道他对它有什么看法。

00:56:56.760 --> 00:57:02.040
<v Tobin Low>I think I was surprised that they were happy at one point. Or like they had a really good relationship.
<v Tobin Low>我想我惊讶的是他们曾经一度很开心。或者他们曾经有一段很好的关系。

00:57:02.040 --> 00:57:03.730
<v Sean Cole>Right.
<v Sean Cole>对。

00:57:03.730 --> 00:57:22.600
<v Tobin Low>I think that I would have assumed that that would survive in some way in the retellings. The original's just kind of sad. And then it becomes extraordinary in a way that like by the end, people's hurt is not the focus.
<v Tobin Low>我想我会假设那会以某种方式在复述中存活下来。原版故事只是有点悲伤。然后它变得非凡，以至于到最后，人们的伤痛不再是焦点。

00:57:22.600 --> 00:57:23.680
<v Sean Cole>Yeah.
<v Sean Cole>是的。

00:57:23.680 --> 00:57:35.560
<v Tobin Low>It's like sort of how wild people's actions are and like you've sort of drifted far away from the original, smaller, more human hurt.
<v Tobin Low>就像人们的行为是多么疯狂，你已经从最初的、更小的、更人性化的伤痛中漂移得很远了。

00:57:35.560 --> 00:57:38.860
<v Sean Cole>And he has a theory as to how they all collectively got there.
<v Sean Cole>他有一个关于他们如何集体走到那一步的理论。

00:57:38.860 --> 00:58:10.170
<v Tobin Low>In retrospect, I think what happened is that everyone knew that there was this thing coming in the story where Kyle was going to leave Elliot. And that's sort of like-- all roads lead to this big moment in the story. And I think to some degree, we were all reverse engineering to that moment. Like how do you explain the beats of what happened? How do you explain everyone's actions, so that that makes sense and lands really hard?
<v Tobin Low>回想起来，我想发生的事情是每个人都知道故事里有这一幕，凯尔要离开埃利奥特。那是……所有的路都通向故事里的这个大时刻。我想在某种程度上，我们都在对那个时刻进行“逆向工程”。你如何解释发生了什么？你如何解释每个人的行为，使之说得通且非常震撼？

00:58:10.170 --> 00:58:10.950
<v Sean Cole>Right.
<v Sean Cole>对。

00:58:10.950 --> 00:58:17.400
<v Tobin Low>So that you recreate the same gasp that you had when you heard it.
<v Tobin Low>这样你就能重现你听到它时的那个惊叹。

00:58:17.400 --> 00:58:18.590
<v Sean Cole>Right.
<v Sean Cole>对。

00:58:18.590 --> 00:58:20.430
<v Tobin Low>So that I can create that moment again.
<v Tobin Low>这样我就能再次创造那个时刻。

00:58:20.430 --> 00:58:25.050
<v Sean Cole>Because in the original, there is no explanation. And you're just kind of like, what the heck?
<v Sean Cole>因为在原版里，没有任何解释。你只是有点像……什么鬼？

00:58:25.050 --> 00:58:25.890
<v Tobin Low>Yes.
<v Tobin Low>是的。

00:58:25.890 --> 00:58:28.500
<v Sean Cole>And that's an uncomfortable feeling.
<v Sean Cole>那是一种不舒服的感觉。

00:58:28.500 --> 00:58:30.420
<v Tobin Low>Right, exactly.
<v Tobin Low>对，完全正确。

00:58:30.420 --> 00:59:07.050
<v Sean Cole>Just like it is in life. But of course, as Tobin had to remind me, the original story is life. That version wasn't part of a game. It really happened. Weirdly, there was only one more major change to the story after Tobin's version. When the comedian Brian Park tells it next, the guy in the TikTok video is not Kyle but one of Kyle's many unsuspecting victims, warning other TikTokers to watch out for this predator, which of course, makes a lot more sense. Oh, and there's no mention of them getting a cat.
<v Sean Cole>就像生活中一样。但当然，正如托宾提醒我的那样，原版故事就是生活。那个版本不是游戏的一部分。它确实发生了。奇怪的是，在托宾的版本后，故事又发生了一个重大改变。当喜剧演员布莱恩·朴（Brian Park）接下来讲述它时，TikTok视频里的家伙不再是凯尔，而是凯尔众多毫无戒心的受害者之一，警告其他TikTok用户提防这个捕食者，这当然更有道理。噢，也没提到他们养猫。

00:59:07.050 --> 00:59:29.190
<v Sean Cole>And then the two versions after that are basically identical to Brian's. It's like there were no more questions to be answered. No gaps in understanding. All of the reverse engineering Tobin talked about was complete, which makes you wonder if there's a natural end to a game like this. If you can only retell the story so many times before it plateaus, stays static.
<v Sean Cole>然后接下来的两个版本基本上和布莱恩的一样。就像没有更多问题需要解答了。理解上没有空白。托宾提到的所有逆向工程都完成了，这让你好奇像这样的游戏是否有自然的终点。如果你只能在它变平稳、保持静态之前复述这个故事这么多遍。

00:59:29.190 --> 00:59:45.730
<v Sean Cole>I bet you that happens to urban myths, too. Alex Sujong Laughlin says watching the story go through all of those changes was kind of thrilling. It was like seeing your own little monster come alive on the laboratory table. But by the last couple two, three versions of the story, she had this other feeling as well.
<v Sean Cole>我敢说都市神话也会发生这种情况。亚历克斯·苏琼·劳克林说，看着故事经历所有那些改变有点惊心动魄。就像看着你自己的一只小怪物在实验室的桌子上活过来。但在故事的最后两三个版本中，她也有了另一种感觉。

00:59:45.730 --> 00:59:47.230
<v Alex Sujong Laughlin>It makes me feel sad.
<v Alex Sujong Laughlin>它让我感到难过。

00:59:47.230 --> 00:59:48.310
<v Sean Cole>Why?
<v Sean Cole>为什么？

00:59:48.310 --> 01:00:16.600
<v Alex Sujong Laughlin>It makes me think of the way that people become caricatures to each other. And it just feels like, you know, I don't know. Not to be all didactic and stuff, but we're talking the night before election day. And I've watched this play out for months, the way that people just get flattened into you know, conservative rednecks who hate everybody or radical feminazis who want to kill babies.
<v Alex Sujong Laughlin>它让我想起人们如何成为彼此的漫画。这感觉就像，你知道，我不知道。不是要讲大道理什么的。但我们正在谈论选举日的前一天。我观看了这几个月里发生的事情，人们被扁平化为你知道的，讨厌所有人的保守派红脖子，或者是想杀掉婴儿的激进女权主义者。

01:00:16.600 --> 01:00:17.110
<v Sean Cole>Right.
<v Sean Cole>对。

01:00:17.110 --> 01:00:23.590
<v Alex Sujong Laughlin>And it just-- it bums me out to see it happen so quickly, even in such a low stakes story.
<v Alex Sujong Laughlin>看到它发生得这么快，即使在一个风险这么低的故事里，也让我很丧。

01:00:23.590 --> 01:00:27.010
<v Sean Cole>It's sort of like all of us are playing the game, all the time.
<v Sean Cole>就像我们所有人一直在玩那个游戏。

01:00:27.010 --> 01:00:49.030
<v Alex Sujong Laughlin>Yeah. And like maybe in those moments when you feel like you want to jump to a conclusion or flatten somebody because it makes the story better, maybe don't. You know? Maybe don't. I don't know. I would like to create fewer of these monster Kyles, even if they're fictional.
<v Alex Sujong Laughlin>是的。就像也许在那些你想跳向结论或贬低某人因为这让故事更好的时刻，也许别这么做。你知道吗？也许别这么做。我不知道。我想创造更少的这些怪物凯尔，即使它们是虚构的。

01:00:49.030 --> 01:01:11.100
<v Sean Cole>That seems like a worthy slogan on behalf of decency and fairness to others. Let's create fewer monster Kyles. Only a handful of people in the world would understand what it means, of course. But just like the juiciest gossip, maybe it would spread.
<v Sean Cole>那似乎是一个代表正派和对他人公平的值得的口号。让我们创造更少的怪物凯尔。当然，世界上只有少数人会明白那意味着什么。但就像最有趣的八卦，也许它会传播。

01:01:11.100 --> 01:01:48.150
<v Ira Glass>Sean Cole is one of the producers of our program. To hear the full episode that Normal Gossip did where all this plays out, you can find that and their other episodes wherever you get your podcasts. By the way, the friends did tell Elliot about the TikTok. He laughed.
<v Ira Glass>肖恩·科尔是我们节目的制作人之一。要听《Normal Gossip》所做的完整剧集以及这一切如何上演，你可以在你获取播客的任何地方找到那一期以及他们的其他剧集。顺便说一句，朋友们确实告诉了埃利奥特那个TikTok。他笑了。

01:01:48.150 --> 01:02:10.530
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced today by Tobin Low. People who put together today's show include Elna Baker, Chris Benderev, Zoe Chace, Sean Cole, Michal Comite, Aviva DeKornfeld, Valerie Kipnis, Stowe Nelson, Katherine Rae Mondo, Nadia Reiman, Ryan Rumery, Charlotte Sleeper, Lilly Sullivan, Frances Swanson, Christopher Swetala, Matt Tierney, Julie Whitaker, and Diane Wu. Our managing editor is Sarah Abdurrahman. Our senior editor is David Kestenbaum. Our executive editor is Emanuele Berry.
<v Ira Glass>我们今天的节目由托宾·洛制作。制作今天节目的包括埃尔纳·贝克（Elna Baker）、克里斯·本德雷夫（Chris Benderev）、佐伊·蔡斯（Zoe Chace）、肖恩·科尔、米哈尔·科米特（Michal Comite）、阿维娃·德科恩菲尔德（Aviva DeKornfeld）、瓦莱丽·基普尼斯（Valerie Kipnis）、斯托·尼尔森（Stowe Nelson）、凯瑟琳·雷·蒙多（Katherine Rae Mondo）、纳迪亚·雷曼（Nadia Reiman）、瑞安·鲁梅里（Ryan Rumery）、夏洛特·斯利珀（Charlotte Sleeper）、莉莉·沙利文（Lilly Sullivan）、弗朗西斯·斯旺森（Frances Swanson）、克里斯托弗·斯韦塔拉（Christopher Swetala）、马特·蒂尔尼（Matt Tierney）、朱莉·惠特克（Julie Whitaker）和黛安·吴。我们的执行编辑是萨拉·阿卜杜拉赫曼（Sarah Abdurrahman）。我们的高级编辑是戴维·克 the stenbaum（David Kestenbaum）。我们的执行编辑是埃马努埃莱·贝里（Emanuele Berry）。

01:02:10.530 --> 01:02:46.530
<v Ira Glass>Special thanks today to John Bistline, Cen Qian, Justin Ellis, Kelsey McKinney, Jae Towle-Viera, and Geoff Triplett. Melissa Lott, our contestant in the climate game show-- she has a podcast of her own, The Big Switch, about the transition to a net zero world. Our website, where right now you can find all kinds of merch-- onesies, t-shirts, sweatshirts, Public Radio tattoos for your holiday shopping-- thisamericanlife.org. This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange. Special thanks as always to our program's co-founder, Mr. Torey Malatia. He's actually on his way to the office right now, probably because I sent him this.
<v Ira Glass>今天特别感谢约翰·比斯特莱恩（John Bistline）、岑谦（Cen Qian）、贾斯汀·埃利斯（Justin Ellis）、凯尔西·麦金尼（Kelsey McKinney）、杰·托尔-维埃拉（Jae Towle-Viera）和杰夫·特里普利特（Geoff Triplett）。梅丽莎·洛特，我们在气候游戏节目中的参赛者……她自己有一个播客《大转换》（The Big Switch），关于向净零世界的转型。我们的网站，现在你可以在那里找到各种周边——连体衣、T恤、卫衣、公共广播纹身，为你的节日购物——thisamericanlife.org。《This American Life》由公共广播交换平台PRX传送给公共广播电台。像往常一样，特别感谢我们节目的联合创始人托里·马拉蒂亚（Torey Malatia）先生。他现在正前往办公室的路上，大概是因为我发了这个给他。

01:02:46.530 --> 01:02:55.560
<v George Gray>Torey Malatia, come on down. You're the next contestant on This American Life.
<v George Gray>托里·马拉蒂亚，快请进。你是《This American Life》的下一位参赛者。

01:02:55.560 --> 01:03:00.560
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week, with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周回来，带来更多《This American Life》的故事。
